    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nA mapping that doesn't edit buffer text doesn't seem cascade with multicursor. This doesn't seem to be mentioned in the `:h multicursor`, while `:h dev-multicursor` has the following sentence:\n\n> Multicursor \"cascade\" is meaningful only for edits and motions.\n\nI don't think this is the case, as there are examples when something that is not an edit or a motion is useful to cascade. Like a mapping that simply logs some value in a Lua table or adds extmark somewhere that depends on cursor position.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create `init.lua` with the following contents:\n\n    ```lua\n    _G.log = {}\n    local ns_id = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('test')\n    local rhs = function()\n      local cursor = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n      vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns_id, cursor[1] - 1, 0, {})\n      table.insert(_G.log, { cursor = cursor })\n    end\n\n    vim.keymap.set('n', 'gh', rhs)\n\n    -- Prepare buffer for easier repro\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { 'aaa', 'bbb', 'ccc' })\n    vim.bo.modified = false\n    ```\n\n2. `nvim --clean -u init.lua`\n3. `vipQ` to initiate multicursor.\n4. `gh` to execute a target mapping.\n5. `:=log` only shows `{ { cursor = { 3, 0 } } }`, i.e. the function was called only once at main cursor.\n6. `:=vim.api.nvim_buf_get_extmarks(0, vim.api.nvim_get_namespaces().test, 0, -1, {})` only shows `{ { 1, 2, 0 } }`, which confirms that the function was called only at main cursor.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIt is possible to cascade a mapping that neither edits text nor moves cursor.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1824+g27ee55c09d\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.2.8-arch1-2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage",
