            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> > a mapping that simply logs some value in a Lua table or adds extmark somewhere        \n>         \n> A workaround is to iterate the cursor extmarks (`nvim.multicursor` namespace) and do stuff at each mark, instead of expecting multicursor to repeat side-effect-only actions.\n\nYeah, I thought about that. It is still not the same. It requires adjusting the mapping under the  assumption that the behavior won't change. Plus it won't work in all reasonable cases:\n- With visual selection there is no established and documented way to get it using only cursor extmarks data.\n- There is no (good) way to get the information about a custom operator that is applied to motion/textobject but doesn't edit text.\n\n> I looked into this, but can't find a coherent rule without regressing other things.\n\nThe current main use case is the \"exchange\" operator: its first application \"records\" a region (while also possibly highlighting it), the second application exchanges the new region with the recorded one. It now has problems with making it work outside of this issue (establishing a robust relation between first and second regions in case of extra cursors), but this issue doesn't even allow the first stage to cascade.\n\nCouple of ideas in the vein of this use case:\n1. Cascade if any extmarks have changed in the current buffer as a result of action at cursor. This seems doable.\n2. Cascade if any user facing Lua data (including plugin local and in function closures) has changed. This seems to be complex to do, but maybe.\n3. Force cascade for any Lua function or `<Cmd>...<CR>`. Not sure how it breaks the current behavior.",
